From: Kamil Bienkiewicz <perceivalpercy@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: qualcomm: ppe: apply the L4 port mangle in packet
 byte order

ppe_offload_apply_l4_mangle() combined the source and destination ports
into a host-order u32 and applied the flow action's mask and value to
it. The flowtable builds those from htonl() values describing the four
header bytes as they sit in the packet, so on a little-endian CPU the
rewrite lands in the wrong half: a masquerade that keeps the client's
port happens to come out right, but one that remaps it is programmed
with the original port. Conntrack only remaps when that public
address:port towards the same destination is already taken, normally by
another LAN client, so the offloaded flow reused that client's NAT
mapping: the server's replies (handled in software) were delivered to
the other client. A LAN host could arrange such a collision on purpose
for fixed-port protocols (NTP, IKE, SIP, WireGuard with a set port) and
receive another client's replies, across VLANs too when they share the
WAN address; for other traffic it broke the connection. It never
accepted inbound traffic conntrack had not approved. Affected: router
mode with PPE hardware flow offload enabled.

Combine and split the ports in their packet byte layout instead, and
decline a flow that also rewrites the destination port: only the
source port is programmed, so such a rewrite would otherwise be
dropped silently.

Found on the bench while testing reply-direction offload, whose
destination-port check tripped over the garbled port. Measured on a
GL-BE9300 with masquerade forced to remap (nft `masquerade to
:61000-61010` for the test port): before, the remapped connection was
offloaded and then carried no data at all (iperf3 hung until killed);
after, 2351 Mbit/s offloaded with the CPU 98.2% idle.

Signed-off-by: Kamil Bienkiewicz <perceivalpercy@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/ppe_offload.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/ppe_offload.c
@@ -276,15 +276,23 @@ struct ppe_offload_l4_ports {
 static int ppe_offload_apply_l4_mangle(struct ppe_offload_l4_ports *ports,
 					const struct flow_action_entry *act)
 {
-	u32 word;
+	__be16 word[2];
+	u32 w;
 
 	if (act->mangle.offset != 0)
 		return -EOPNOTSUPP;
 
-	word = (__force u32)ports->source << 16 | (__force u32)ports->dest;
-	word = (word & act->mangle.mask) | act->mangle.val;
-	ports->source = (__force __be16)(word >> 16);
-	ports->dest = (__force __be16)(word & 0xffff);
+	/* The mangle's mask and value describe the four header bytes as
+	 * they sit in the packet (source port, then destination port), so
+	 * combine them in that byte layout, not as a host-order integer.
+	 */
+	word[0] = ports->source;
+	word[1] = ports->dest;
+	memcpy(&w, word, sizeof(w));
+	w = (w & act->mangle.mask) | act->mangle.val;
+	memcpy(word, &w, sizeof(w));
+	ports->source = word[0];
+	ports->dest = word[1];
 
 	return 0;
 }
@@ -639,6 +647,14 @@ static int ppe_offload_flow_replace(stru
 		return ppe_offload_decline("not snat-outbound (src %pI4->%pI4, dst %pI4->%pI4)",
 					    &match_src, &new_src, &match_dst, &new_dst);
 
+	/* Only the source port is programmed (nat_l4_port below); a flow
+	 * that also rewrites the destination port would be forwarded to the
+	 * wrong one, so fail closed instead of dropping the rewrite.
+	 */
+	if (l4.dest != match_dport)
+		return ppe_offload_decline("destination port rewrite (%u->%u) unsupported",
+					    be16_to_cpu(match_dport), be16_to_cpu(l4.dest));
+
 	/* eth.bytes is {h_dest[6], h_source[6]}. The new source half
 	 * must be this egress port's own MAC -- if it isn't, either
 	 * this isn't the flow we think it is or the port's L3_IF entry
